# Marquise Cut Tension Setting Ring ({{year}}): 4 Risk Factors Buyers Miss

![Three loose marquise cut diamonds held in jeweller tweezers against a soft grey backdrop as the DiamondCritics featured image for the {{year}} marquise cut tension setting ring guide titled 4 risk factors buyers miss](/images/marquise-tension-setting-ring.jpg "Marquise Cut Tension Setting Ring 4 Risk Factors Buyers Miss")

## TL;DR — Marquise Tension Setting Ring in One Scroll

**A tension setting holds the diamond in place by pure pressure from two sides of the ring band — no prongs, no bezel, no basket.** The stone appears to float suspended in the metal. For round diamonds the look is dramatic and the engineering works. For marquise diamonds the engineering fails. The two sharp pointed tips are the exact wrong contact points to absorb thousands of pounds of inward pressure. True tension-set marquise rings are nearly non-existent in the certified market — and for good mechanical reasons.

**The 4 risk factors buyers miss on marquise tension settings (all 4 covered in full below):**

- **Risk 1 — The two sharp tips cannot take the pressure.** Tension settings exert 60–100 lbs of inward force on the stone. On a round, that force spreads across the smooth girdle. On a marquise, the force concentrates at the two sharp points — the weakest spot on the stone.
- **Risk 2 — Zero backup if tension fails.** No prongs means no safety backup. If the metal fatigue causes even a 0.1 mm drift in the band, the stone falls out. No warning, no secondary catch.
- **Risk 3 — Resizing is impossible without re-setting the stone.** The tension force depends on the band's exact circumference. Change the size, change the pressure — the stone loosens or cracks. Resize means complete re-build.
- **Risk 4 — The certified market does not stock true tension-set marquise.** The three major online retailers list zero pure tension-set marquise rings. The closest alternatives are bypass pavé kite-set and diamond vine side-stone designs — these give the "floating stone" look without the mechanical risk.

→ Bypass pavé pick: **[Bypass Pavé Kite-Set in 14k Yellow Gold at $1,740](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/bypass-pave-kite-set-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-by-james-allen-item-304940?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** — bypass shank gives the tension-adjacent "floating" look with safe prong hold.
→ White gold bypass: **[Bypass Pavé Kite-Set in 14k White Gold at $1,740](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/bypass-pave-kite-set-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-304939?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** — same bypass design in white gold.
→ Vine side-stone pick: **[Diamond Vine Side Stone in Platinum at $6,335](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/diamond-vine-side-stone-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-304649?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** — vine side stones create a tension-illusion in platinum.

**Priority:** if you are searching for "marquise tension setting ring," the honest answer is: don't. Buy the bypass pavé or vine side-stone instead. The visual effect is similar — the mechanical risk is zero. See the full [marquise cut diamond guide](/marquise-cut-diamond) for shape fundamentals.

