## How to Verify a Marquise Tension-Adjacent Ring Before You Buy

Three checks every ring must pass before you commit.

**Check 1 — Confirm the setting is bypass pavé kite-set or vine side-stone, not true tension.** True tension-set marquise should not be in the retailer's listing. If it is, walk — the engineering risk is too high.

**Check 2 — Confirm the centre stone hold is prong or vine, not pressure.** The 360° video should show visible prongs on the centre stone basket. If no prongs are visible and the stone appears to float with no metal contact, it is a true tension setting — avoid.

**Check 3 — Confirm the setting resizes at standard rates.** Ask the retailer the resize cost for ±1 size. Standard answer should be $30–$80 (often free). If the quote is $200+, the setting is more complex than a bypass — likely a true tension setting under a different name. [IGI](https://www.igi.org/) and [GIA 4Cs](https://4cs.gia.edu/) both certify loose stones only — the setting type is on the retailer's listing.

I run these three checks before I quote any client on a tension-adjacent ring. The bypass and vine designs give the floating-stone aesthetic safely. True tension setting for marquise is a category to avoid entirely.

